==Gravitational Dynamics==

The Aurelius system is governed by a series of complex orbital resonances and gravitational interactions. The most significant of these is the relationship between [[Quetzal]] and the inner terrestrial planets. Quetzal acts as a gravitational anchor, clearing the inner system of debris but also subjecting [[Adonia]] and [[Mictlán]] to intense tidal forces. 

In the outer system, the relationship between [[Alcázar]], [[Lucero]], and [[Centinela]] maintains the stability of the Kuiper-type debris field. The 98-degree tilt of Alcázar is theorized to have been caused by a massive protoplanetary collision during the system's early formation, which may have also perturbed the orbit of [[Erebo]] into its current eccentric path.

==Cultural Significance==

The naming of the planets reflects the three primary cultures of the Adonian world. Insulonian (Spanish) names are used for primary geographical and mythological references, while Sanese (Japanese) names are standard in astronomical and scientific catalogs. Sinopian (Greek) etymology is often found in the historical and theological roots of the system's nomenclature.

